        "t1": "#include <stdio.h>\n\nint main() {\n    int n;\n    scanf(\"%d\", &n);\n\n    /* The range is 1..n inclusive, so the test is i <= n - using i < n would\n       stop one short and never print n itself. */\n    for (int i = 1; i <= n; i++) {\n        /* A space goes before every number except the first, so the output\n           has no trailing space and still separates the numbers. */\n        if (i > 1) printf(\" \");\n        printf(\"%d\", i);\n    }\n    printf(\"\\n\");\n    return 0;\n}\n"
      },
      "answers": {
        "q1": "Use for when the number of iterations is known in advance; use while when it is not known in advance.",
        "q2": "The boundary is included or excluded wrongly. Decide the range in English first, then write that range down as a comment.",
